      "latency_ms": 2801.4,
      "prompt_tokens": 11,
      "completion_tokens": 590,
      "output": "At its most fundamental level, **water** is a chemical compound and the most abundant substance on the surface of Earth. It is essential for all known forms of life. \n\nHere is a breakdown of what water is, explained through different lenses:\n\n### 1. The Chemical View\n* **Chemical Formula:** $\\text{H}_2\\text{O}$\n* **Composition:** Each molecule of water is made up of **two hydrogen atoms** bonded to **one oxygen atom**.\n* **States of Matter:** Water is unique because it is the only natural substance on Earth that exists abundantly in three physical states:\n  * **Liquid:** (The water we drink, rivers, oceans)\n  * **Solid:** (Ice, snow, glaciers)\n  * **Gas:** (Water vapor, steam, humidity in the air)\n\n### 2. Physical Properties\nWater has several remarkable properties that make it unique and vital for life:\n* **The \"Universal Solvent\":** Water can dissolve more substances than any other liquid. This is how plants absorb nutrients and how our blood delivers minerals through our bodies.\n* **High Heat Capacity:** Water can absorb and release large amounts of heat, which helps regulate the temperature of the Earth's climate and the internal temperature of living organisms.\n* **Cohesion and Adhesion:** Water molecules like to stick to each other (cohesion—creating surface tension, allowing insects to walk on water) and to other surfaces (adhesion—allowing water to travel up the roots of tall trees).\n* **Expansion when Frozen:** Unlike most substances, water expands and becomes less dense when it freezes, which is why ice floats on water rather than sinking. If ice sank, lakes and oceans would freeze solid from the bottom up, destroying aquatic life.\n\n### 3. The Ecological and Geological View\n* **Earth’s Coverage:** About **71% of the Earth's surface** is covered by water, mostly in oceans and seas (which hold about 96.5% of all Earth's water).\n* **The Water Cycle (Hydrologic Cycle):** Water is constantly moving. It evaporates from the oceans into the atmosphere, condenses into clouds, falls back to Earth as precipitation (rain or snow), and flows through rivers back to the sea. \n\n### 4. The Biological View\n* **The Basis of Life:** Water is the primary building block of living things. \n* **Human Body:** The human body is made up of about **60% water**. It acts as a lubricant for joints, regulates body temperature, and flushes out waste. Without water, human cells cannot function, and a person can only survive for a few days without it.\n\nIn summary, water is a simple molecule with extraordinary properties that make it the driving force of nature, weather, and life itself."
